          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ForTopologicalSpaces"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#generation"} Generating topologies
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ForSites"} Relation to Grothendieck topologies and coverages
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#literature"} Literature
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em base
            and a
            %em subbase
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            are ways of generating its topology from something simpler. This is the application to topology of the general concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base"} base
          %h2#ForTopologicalSpaces Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            be its collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            (its ‘topology’).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong base
              or
              %strong basis
              for (or “of”)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subset \tau
              – whose members are called
              %strong basic open subsets
              or
              %strong generating open subsets
              – such that every open subset is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
              of basic ones.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong subbase
              for (or “of”)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset \tau
              – whose members are called
              %strong subbasic open subsets
              – such that every open subset is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+intersections"} finitary intersections
              of subbasic ones.
          %p
            If only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is given, then a
            %strong base
            or
            %strong subbase
            on this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is any collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that is a base or subbase for
            %em some
            topology on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a{:href => "#generation"} below
            for a characterisation of which collections these can be.
          %p
            Now fix a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong local base
              or
              %strong base of neighborhoods
              or
              %strong fundamental system of neighborhoods
              for (or “of”)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is a subcollection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subset \tau
              – whose members are called
              %strong basic neighborhoods
              or
              %strong generating neighborhoods
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              – such that every basic neighborhood of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhood"} neighborhood
              and every neighborhood of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superset"} superset
              of some basic neighborhood.
          %p We may also allow basic neighborhoods to be non-open, but this really doesn't make any difference; any local base may be refined to a local base of open neighborhoods, and most local bases in practices already come that way.
          %p
            A
            %strong local subbase
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is a family of neighbourhoods
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            such that each neighbourhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            contains a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            of elements of the family.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              The minimum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
              of a base of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              %strong weight
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is the
              %strong character
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
              of the characters at all points of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              %strong character
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            We have assumed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            to simplify the description of this concept; but in general one must speak of classes of cardinalities rather than individual cardinalities.
          %p
            If the character of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            satisfies the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-countable+space"} first axiom of countability
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            satisfies the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-countable+space"} second axiom of countability
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
              on a set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
              subsets is a base, and the singleton
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\}
              is a local base at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
              discrete spaces are second-countable.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For every
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+space"} paracompact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              that are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              forms a base for the topology. (For instance, a base for the topology on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              is given by the collection of open intervals
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \subset \mathbb{R}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This means that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
              families consisting of such
              %em basic
              open subsets are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+covers"} good open covers
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              Refining the previous example, every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              has a basis consisting of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              with
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
              radius. (For instance, a base for the topology on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              is given by the collection of open intervals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \subset \mathbb{R}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo −
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b - a
              is rational.) Similarly, the collection of open balls with rational radius containing a given point is a local base at that point. Therefore, every metric space is first-countable.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              Now consider a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable+space"} separable
              metric space; that is, we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subset"} dense subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              which is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
              radius and centres in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              is given by the collection of open intervals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \subset \mathbb{R}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              are rational.) Therefore, every separable metric space is second-countable.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#generation Generating topologies
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be simply a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          #Recognition.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (recognition of topological bases)
            %p
              A collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a base for
              %em some
              topology on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              iff these conditions are met:
            %ul
              %li
                The elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                cover
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %li
                For any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, V \in B
                and any point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U \cap V
                there is a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \in B
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subseteq U \cap V
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in W
          %p
            These conditions amount to saying that for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\in B
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in U
            is a base for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (which is then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhood"} neighborhood
            filter of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %em Every
              collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              of subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a subbase for some topology on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            A subbase naturally generates a base (for the same topology) by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+closure"} closing
            it under finitary intersections. (The resulting base will actually be closed under intersection.)
          %h3#ForSites Relation to Grothendieck topologies and coverages
          %p
            If one thinks of the topology on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as being encoded in the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            that it induces on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
          %p
            This coverage is
            %em not
            in general a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis+for+the+Grothendieck+topology"} basis for the Grothendieck topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            with arbitrary
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            (here, closed under these intersections) and transitive. Unfortunately the established terminology “basis” in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            is not quite consistent with the inclusion of topological spaces into topos theory: “basis” in topology corresponds to “coverage” in topos theory, not to “basis” in topos theory.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood+base"} neighbourhood base
          %h2#literature Literature
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ryszard+Engelking"}> Ryszard Engelking
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em General Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 6
                %a{:href => "https://www.heldermann.de/SSPM/SSPM06/sspm06.htm"} ISBN 388538-006-4
            %li
              %p Parimal, Proofs (?), Lecture 13: Basis for a topology
